Q1. What are all the applications you've migrated from IBM to Azure Cloud.

Answer: We have migrated Compute, Network, Storage, IAM, Monitoring.
In Compute, we migrated from Virtual Servers to Virtual Machines
In Network, we have migrated from Virtual Private Cloud to Virtual Network
In Storage, we migrated from IBM Cloud Storage Object to Azure Blob Storage
In IAM, we migrated IBM Cloud IAM to Azure AD + IAM ROles
In Monitoring, we migrated from IBM Log Analytics to Azure Monitor

Q2. What exactly you do with 20+ resources which you've setup and managing.?

Answer: We are managing our environment with multiple Azure Resources such as Virtual Machines, Virtual Network, Virtual Machine Scale Set, Network Security Groups, Storage Accounts, Identity and Access Management (IAM)
Virtual Machines will provision with right size, attach OS Disks or Data Disks, Configure Availability Sets and Availability Zones, Apply NSGs.
Virtual Network creates network with CIDR blocks, Isolates our Azure resources from Public Internet, Allows VMs and databases to communicate privately.
Virtual Machine Scale Set acts as a HPA in OpenShift. It will automatically increases/decreases the VMs based on the spike. It has scale-out rule and scale-in rule. Scale-out rule helps increasing the VMs if the utilization limit exceeds. Scale-in rule helps decreasing the VMs if the VM is under utilized.
Network Security Groups will control inbound and outbound traffic. It will also create inbound and outbound rules to allow/deny traffic based on source, destination, port, protocol(TCP/UDP/IP) and action(allow/deny).
Storage Accounts which stores all the data and services together. It will also set access tiers and enables soft delete. In Azure, we have 5 storage accounts, Blob storage, Disk Storage, FIle Storage, Queue Storage, Table Storage.
Identity and Access Management (IAM) is managing under Authentication and Authorization. Authentication will verify who you are and Authorization will decide what action you are allowed to do.

Q3. Difference Between Public and Private Subnets.

Answer: Public subnet is Open to Internet and has Public IPs. Some LB and Bastion Hosts lives here. Public Subnet is for web frontend.
Private Subnet is isolated. Database and Services can communicate privately. VMs and Databases have private subnets.

Q4. In Azure, Web application frontend is showing session timeout error. How would you troubleshoot.?
Answer: First, I will acknowledge the issue. Then I'll go check pod logs in Datadog/Splunk, ath token expiry date, idle timeout settings, readiness and liveness probes, NSG Rules to find if there any return traffic bloackage, Test the connectivity, Verify TLS Cert expiration details.

Q5. What are the Azure Container Apps.?
Answer: Basically It runs containers on top of K8s, but we no need to manage the kubernetes API at all as the containers are serverless.
It has serverless containers, built-in KEDA-based autoscaling. It will scale containers to zero when there is no traffic. It also has built-in Ingress and built-in LoadBalancing which automatically distributes incoming traffic.

Q6. Deploy a new CICD Application in Azure.
Answer: To deploy a new application, we can folow GitOps method.
We will write a code and push it to Git. 
Create Azure Subscription and Resource Group, Azure Container Registry to store Docker Images, Azure Key Vault to store secrets.
Then create Pipeline for build and deploy the application, Run Unit Tests and Build Docker Image.
Update the manifest with new image tag and commit the changes. 
ArgoCD detects the change and sync it with desired state of Git. Alternatively, Azure DevOps will deploy directly using oc apply command
Once the applciation is deployed, verify the pod, services, routes.
And continue monitor the application using Azure Monitor., Azure Log Analytics.

Q7. What are the Azure Services.?
In Azure, we have Compute, Storage, Networking, IAM, Monitoring.
Under Compute, we have Virtual Machines, Virtual Machine Scale Set, AKS, Azure Functions, Azure App Services.
Under Storage, we have Blob storage, Disk Storage, FIle Storage, Queue Storage, Table Storage.
Under Networking, we have Virtual Network(VNet), Network Security Groups(NSG), Load Balancer, VPN, DNS, Private Endpoints.
Under IAM, we have Azure Active Directory, RBAC, Key Vault, Azure Policy.
Under Monitoring, we have Azure Monitor, Log Analytics.
